pageType: content
background: { type: solid, color: "$paper" }
notes: |
  Slide 7: Distance, Redshift, and Standard Sirens: Measuring Cosmic Expansion.
  - Explains how gravitational-wave binary inspirals function as self-calibrating "standard sirens" (Schutz 1986): general relativity directly predicts the absolute strain amplitude from the observed chirp rate without requiring any astronomical distance ladder.
  - Reviews the Hubble Constant tension: Planck cosmic microwave background (67.4 +/- 0.5 km/s/Mpc) vs SH0ES local Cepheid/SN Ia distance ladder (73.0 +/- 1.0 km/s/Mpc) represents a ~5-sigma discrepancy.
  - Discusses GW170817 standard siren measurement (H0 = 70.0 +12.0 -8.0 km/s/Mpc) and statistical "dark siren" methodology using GLADE+ galaxy catalog cross-correlations.
  - Highlights the distance-inclination degeneracy: binary orbital inclination angle i is partially degenerate with distance D_L in two-polarization strain measurements.
